{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>If a matter is hidden.</b> It is covered from verses that have two aspects in their interpretation, for we find כי יפליא נדר, כי יפליא לנדור, whose interpretation is the language of distinguishing — like דשן / לדשנו, מחלצות / ואחלצה צוררי ריקם, and so many others." "<b>From you a matter.</b> The judge is to separate a matter, even if the ruling is hidden from you — that came before you to put the dead to death by means of the testimony of two or three. Therefore they juxtaposed it here." "<b>Between blood and blood, between nega and nega.</b> Jerusalem Targum: between blood and blood — between blood of slaughter and blood of virginity. And between נגע and נגע — between a closed נגע and an open נגע. קטול״א is a wound." "<b>[</b>This I found in the margin: 'כי יפלא ממך דבר בין דם לדם' etc. And we derive that it speaks both of nega'im of a person and of nega'im of garments. And we say in tractate Sanhedrin: a zaken mamreh — only when he rebels in a matter whose intentional violation is karet and whose unintentional violation is a sin-offering. And we raise the question on this: with nega'im you can find a case whose intentional violation is karet. Now this is difficult: well and good, בין דם לדם — you can find it, that he declared blood of niddah clean. בין נגע לנגע — with nega'im of a person you can find it, that a kohen declared a metzora clean and he then performed divine service, which is in karet. And likewise with nega'im of houses. But nega'im of garments — how do you find it? If you say that he declared clean a garment that has a nega, and a kohen who had a nega performed divine service — there is no tum'ah that is in karet, for tum'ah is in karet only when it issues from his body, like one who enters the Temple with a sheretz in his hand, for which he receives only lashes. One may answer that the matter depends on what we say in the Mekhilta in tractate Nazir: a garment that has a nega is forbidden for benefit, and since it is forbidden for benefit it is not worth a perutah. And so you can find it, for example, if a man betrothed a woman with a garment that has a nega — and by the oral law kiddushin takes effect from another. And there is a dispute between the sages and the zaken mamreh: the sages declare the garment tamei and forbidden for benefit and betrothed to the second man, and it becomes karet for the first man, for she is an married woman to him, or vice versa. Thus far the gloss.]"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {3 "The entire comment [3] is presented as a marginal gloss (הגהה) that Chizkuni reports finding; I have rendered it as his text presents it. The siglum ע״כ הגה״ה at the end means 'end of the gloss.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:20.324841667Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}